Title: memory-capture project key: generic colon sed eats non-default ports, colliding distinct origins
Created: 2026-10-02
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-10-02T09:32Z MinhHaDuong created

--- body ---
## Context

Found by the openrouter-frontier external reviewer seat during the ticket-1008
raid's gaze run on PR #1121 (2026-10-02), dispositioned out-of-diff there and
verified on main afterwards. `scripts/memory-capture.sh` `project_key()` derives
the private-capture key directory from the origin URL. Its comment states "a
non-default port stays and makes the identity distinct", and the sed strips
only the default ports (`:22/`, `:443/`, `:80/`) — but the generic rule that
follows (`-e 's|:\([^/]\)|/\1|'`, scripts/memory-capture.sh:62) rewrites every
remaining colon to a slash.

Consequence: `host:2222/path` and `host/2222/path` (two distinct origins — a
non-default SSH port versus an org-or-owner path segment named `2222`) hash to
the same project key. Private captures for distinct projects then share one
age key under `~/.config/keys/memory/`, contradicting the documented identity
requirement and the function's own comment.

## Actions

1. Decide the identity rule: either honor the comment (keep non-default ports
   distinct — remove the generic colon rule and handle the remaining
   scp-like `git@host:path` case explicitly), or update the comment and accept
   the collision, documenting why.
2. Add a test to tests/test_memory_capture.sh covering the two-origins case
   (`host:2222/path` vs `host/2222/path`) pinning whichever rule is chosen.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] project_key() behavior matches its comment, or the comment matches the
      behavior, with the decision recorded here
- [ ] A regression test pins the distinctness (or the accepted collision) of
      port-carrying vs slash-carrying origins
